Chapter: DeskMenuClickTest
  cites Works chapter GopDesk
  cites Works chapter GopPrograms
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A start-menu row answers its own pane, and this is the guard that says so.

 It was written to chase a reported defect, that clicking System Info opened
 the Program Runner. **That report was the harness's fault and not the
 desk's**: the driving timeline moved the pointer 112 device pixels in ONE
 mouse sample, that sample was not delivered on the failing boot, and the
 pointer was still two rows down on the Program Runner when the click landed.
 Splitting the move into four small samples opens the Monitor every time. The
 arm is kept because the question it asks is worth a runner either way.

 THE POINT COMES FROM THE LAID TREE, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THIS AN ARM RATHER
 THAN A RESTATEMENT. A capture can show the pointer on a row and the click
 still open something else, and no screenshot can say which of the two is
 wrong. Here the click point is READ OFF the same layout the paint uses:
 `dk-menu-laid` answers the tree, `widget-find` answers the row's rectangle,
 and the centre of that rectangle is the point handed to `dk-menu-hit`. So
 the arm clicks exactly what it photographs, and a disagreement can only be
 between the layout and the hit.

 The bounds are LOGICAL and `dk-menu-hit` takes DEVICE, which it divides by
 `ui-wscale` again. That round trip is deliberate: it is the one the desk
 performs, so a scale fault shows here rather than being cancelled out.

 THE EXPECTED ANSWER COMES FROM A DIFFERENT SOURCE THAN THE QUESTION. The
 row is found by its id and the answer is compared against that entry's own
 `ge-scan` from `gpr-entries`, so an arm that read both from the same place
 could not fail. A heading is asked for too, because a heading must answer a
 GROUP code and never a scancode, and the two namespaces sharing a prefix is
 exactly how a row would answer a heading's question.

Section: Body

  dmc-yn : Boolean -> Text
  dmc-yn (v) = if v then "PASS" else "FAIL"

 The centre of a laid rectangle in device pixels.

  dmc-cx : LayoutRect, Integer -> Integer
  dmc-cx (b) (s) = (b.lr-x + b.lr-w / 2) * s

  dmc-cy : LayoutRect, Integer -> Integer
  dmc-cy (b) (s) = (b.lr-y + b.lr-h / 2) * s

 One row: find it, click its centre, and say what answered.

  dmc-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont, Integer -> Text
  dmc-row (w) (h) (ps) (ds) (i) (tf) (s) =
    let e = gpr-at i
    in let laid = dk-menu-laid w h ps ds tf
    in when widget-find laid ("gpr-" & e.ge-label)
      is None -> "  " & e.ge-label & " : not laid"
      is Just (n) ->
        let b = n.wn-bounds
        in let got = dk-menu-hit w h ps (dmc-cx b s) (dmc-cy b s) ds tf
        in "  " & e.ge-label & " : wants " & integer-to-text (e.ge-scan) & " answered " & integer-to-text got & " " & dmc-yn (got == e.ge-scan)

  dmc-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, GopFont, Integer -> [Console] Integer
  dmc-rows (w) (h) (ps) (ds) (i) (tf) (s) =
    if i >= gpr-count then act 0 end
    else act
      print-line-uni (dmc-row w h ps ds i tf s)
      dmc-rows w h ps ds (i + 1) tf s
    end

 THE SECOND PASS CARRIES A FACE, AND THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE THE FIRST ONE
 CANNOT SEE. A faceless lay answers the tree unchanged out of `comp-fit-text`,
 so the first pass grades arithmetic the shipping desk never runs: the desk
 loads CMUNSS and every label is measured. A `GopFont` is a plain record and
 `gf-ok` is what the fit consults, so a synthetic one with a fixed cell puts
 the faced path under the same census without a volume, a glyph or a screen.
 If the two passes disagree, the face is the fault; if they agree, the face is
 exonerated and the fault is downstream of this function entirely.

  dmc-gw : Integer = 9
  dmc-gh : Integer = 18

  dmc-font : Integer -> GopFont
  dmc-font (b) =
    GopFont { gf-ok = True, gf-base = b, gf-gw = dmc-gw, gf-gh = dmc-gh, gf-asc = dmc-gh, gf-cap = dmc-gh }

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let ps = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let w = 1600
    in let h = 900
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let tf = gfont-none
    in let fb = alloc-zeroed (95 * dmc-gw * dmc-gh + 95 + 95) 64
    in let ff = dmc-font fb
    in let sel = gpr-set-sel ps 13
    in let laid = dk-menu-laid w h ps ds tf
    in let hd = when widget-find laid (gpr-group-prefix & "Settings")
      is None -> 0 - 1
      is Just (n) -> dk-menu-hit w h ps (dmc-cx (n.wn-bounds) s) (dmc-cy (n.wn-bounds) s) ds tf
    in act
      print-line-uni ("scale is " & integer-to-text s & ", selection is " & integer-to-text (gpr-sel ps))
      print-line-uni "FACELESS, each visible row clicked at the centre of its own laid rectangle:"
      r <- dmc-rows w h ps ds 0 tf s
      print-line-uni "WITH A FACE, the same census through comp-fit-text:"
      r2 <- dmc-rows w h ps ds 0 ff s
      print-line-uni ("a heading answers a GROUP code and not a scancode: " & dmc-yn (hd >= dk-sc-group))
      print-line-uni ("the heading answered " & integer-to-text hd)
    end
